 can see in the upper right hand  corner of the spread  of the spectrum the end residue spelled  penny k  hence the name but this is an important  peptide because you'll see the ends here  are the asparagine residues and each of  these asparagine residues can deamidate  so this is that post-translational  modification that i mentioned earlier  that has the nh2 group going to an oh  group  so we see moving into this figure the  unmodified  arrow points to the parent peptide but  when you have that nh2 to oh you  actually gain one dalton  mass so now if you look at the d329 for  example  you can actually see that that signal  has shifted a half of a dalton forward  this is because we're looking at a  doubly charged peptide in this  particular case  what we can actually do is do an  extracted ion chromatogram  of that section and you can actually see  the relative abundance of the various  peaks so these are  the iso d or the isospartic acid  diluting before the unmodified peak  the unmodified and then various  combinations of deamidation  of individual residues combined residues  in the double demitation for example  so we can now associate these relative  abundances on a very  mass specific level you can actually  quantify the iso-aspartic acid the  unmodified in each of the individual  deamidation events  and we can do that based on mass as  opposed to just a  signal signal associated with the uv so  we can actually get much higher  selectivity for our quantification  to again monitor individual attributes  of this peptide  much more selectively than we could with  uv  analysis  so then we can do new and change peak  detection that i mentioned before  so once we have those bounding boxes in  the previous slide we can actually  quantify  again on routine samples and test  samples how much of the material has  actually deamidated  and in which fashion but we don't want  to use  we're then having bounding boxes for  example in the reference slide here  we're not actually using the entirety of  this space right there's a lot of white  space where we don't have any signals  so the question then comes up if we're  not monitoring these individual bounding  boxes  what happens if something we're not  expecting pops up at a different mass to  charge  so we have a process related impurity or  product related impurity  in this case we do a direct comparison  using software that essentially  subtracts  the test sample from the reference  sample and we can have it look without  any preconceived notion of the sequence  or the identity of that protein  and say in your reference versus your  test sample has a signal actually  changed  so this is basically asking are there  any new peaks or did any of the peaks  that are in the bounding boxes changed  that we may not be selectively  monitoring in our report so this really  is an agnostic  new or change peak detection it allows  you to identify differences in the  sample  that if they are actually observed you  can then go in with an additional  experiment  to elucidate the identity of those  changes in more detail  so as you can see this is an extremely  complicated method there's a lot of data  processing a lot of data acquisition  that goes into  proper performance of the  multi-attribute method  so what we wanted to do was really  evaluate how well this assay is being  performed across  industry but also identify areas that we  can work together to improve the method  to really allow implementation in both  the product development or process  development side  and hopefully eventually into the  quality control environment  so with this we worked with rich rogers  uh now with bristol meyer squibb to  organize  a combined multi-attribute method  so trina muschauer is a colleague of  mine at nist that's been leading this  study from the nist side and then again  richard  on the bms side has also been helping  along with the mam consortium  which we recruited approximately 30  members throughout industry  to essentially run the same  multi-attribute method  come back together discuss their roles  results  and understand what our current  capabilities are for performing this  analytical method  so the scope of the study again we  really wanted to try to harmonize as  many  aspects of this to really kind of hone  in on the data acquisition component  so in the design we actually did the  digestion at nist to ensure that there  wasn't lab to lab  irreproducibility there so every lab was  actually sent pre-digested samples  shown in the upper left-hand corner we  actually defined the column and gradient  in the blue boxes so each analytical map  each analytical lab  i use the exact same uh plc column and  perform the same gradient  what they were allowed to do was choose  their own equipment so they're able to  use their own uhplc  their own high resolution mass  spectrometer  and you can see on the right hand side  that we actually got a pretty good  sampling of different types of mass  spectrometers  we had q-tops linear ion trap over traps  and  quadrupole-based orbit traps so we tried  to  again limit the variability as much as  we could control and leave it to just  the acquisition and data processing  to understand how this assay was being  performed  and in order to do that we supplied the  users a number of samples  so we supplied them a system suitability  sample  which was basically just a mixture of  individual peptides that are synthetic  that we knew the individual identity of  those peptides  we also supplied them a reference sample  so this is essentially just the digested  mismat  reference material and then we sent them  a couple of these challenge samples to  actually look at their new peak  detection capabilities  so there were three individual samples  one that was a spike sample that had the  15 peptides from the pierce mix  spiked in we also did a stressed  material where the nist map was stressed  with ph to induce high levels of  deamidation  and then we gave them an unknown sample  which was actually just the reference  sample  but on labeled as an unknown to test for  the potential for  false positives so they won't be able to  go through the entirety of this study  today  i can give you a very brief synopsis of  the results and  pointing to the paper that we've  submitted that should be available  in a few months looking at the bottom  right hand corner here we're actually  seeing the results from each of the  individual participants  so each shape on the x-axis represents  an individual pharma lab that  producer analysis and what we can see in  the new peak detection of this spikes  sample is that almost every lab was able  to identify all  15 peptides but some of the labs  identified additional peptides and some  didn't  so some labs found things like modified  spike peptides and spike peptide  impurities  some of these we actually did know are  present from the synthetic  peptide uh synthesis  but they're very very low abundance so  again it really does depend on the  thresholds that you're setting of  whether or not you detect those peptides  in addition there were some nist map  peptides that were detected as either  new or changed  so again this shouldn't have happened in  the individual laboratories but  there was a an issue with column  carryover incomplete washing of some of  the  column components or the autosampler  components etc  that we were able to identify then there  were some unknown m overseas  some of these were due to water  contamination system contamination  uh previous samples etc so while this la  uh  this figure shows that there was some  inconsistencies between the lab there's  a lot of really really good news here  and most everyone was able to identify  all 15 peptides  people were actually setting their new  peak detection criteria within  the current metrics of how  instrumentation was performing  and this was actually based on our  system suitability performance across  the individual labs  and most importantly those that were  deemed false positives or false  negatives could actually be  explained so in the paper we'll actually  go through some of those individual  nuances  where we were able to dig deeper into  the raw data and find some of those  false negatives and false positives and  then some recommendations for  how to go about watching out for these  potential pitfalls that  one could run into so really the future  perspective of mam i think is a very  bright  potential for an analytical assay there  will  definitely be some need for additional  validation criteria a very  good system suitability controls but we  really do believe that this  analytical method has a bright future  and we'll start to see a more broad  implementation of this throughout the  drug development life cycle  so sort of a conclusion on uh nist's uh  kind of current and future state um  obviously we've done a lot of work with  monoclonal antibodies over uh the past  eight or nine years  glycosylation analysis mass spectral  libraries  you name it there's been a lot of  measurements done not to support the  biopharmaceutical specifically  monoclonal antibody field  going from reference materials and now  all the way up into the process  analytics in the upper right hand side  and with that we'd like to continue to  look at other modalities we've actually  started programs in both cartoon  therapies  encapsulated gene therapy mimetic  products  and we're also getting into the rna and  dna-based vaccines  so we really do have a vision as a  national resource  that can provide again the materials in  the science and technology development  to support these emerging modalities and  also the  modalities that have become commonplace  and absolutely necessary  for a solid health care infrastructure  so as a global summary of the nist map  again we really focused on material  properties in releasing this reference  material  there's a very large structural  knowledge base available from nist  as well as 20 plus publications from  stakeholders that have been  put into literature since the material  has been released it's free of  intellectual property so again  anything collected on this material can  in fact be published  it's completely open innovation for  de-risking analytical technology  and it is a perfect common framework for  harmonization  and really understanding across industry  and global  trends and analytical measurements so  just as a reiteration as well we have a  program far beyond maps  we have standards for characterization  that we're developing for a variety of  different  therapeutic modalities and really we're  working on a lot of advanced  analytical characterization standards  etc that can really  be used in the future i think to  characterize these products  and then i'll mention last but not least  that industry challenges really do drive  the science technology and reference  materials will be developed  so there is a robust pandemic response  as well with the current situation  with covet 19 of nist looking at  measurement infrastructure standards and  technology development  that are needed to support uh that field  so with that i'd like to thank a very  large number of folks that have  contributed to this project  over the past year uh the data presented  today is probably 300 plus scientists  throughout industry  that have contributed in some way shape  or form but specifically i'd like to uh  thank the team that is currently working  on some of the mass spectrometry and lc  characterization  uh the biopharma iqs team uh the nist  map team that did a wide variety of  analytical characterization  on the nisnab material and then  specifically call out  our new peak detection round robin  organizers uh risk roger  rich rogers of just now bms uh  and trina mushauer of nist who has been  really leading that effort  so with that i'm very thankful for the  opportunity and i'll look  forward to receiving any questions or  comments that anyone may have  thank you very much
 

